WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.250
Hello, Steve here again, Steve Kaufmann.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:06.300
And today I have the pleasure of
speaking with an online teacher of

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.380
Russian, Daria of Real Russian club.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.920
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.360
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.350
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:20.220
Okay.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.360
Daria, I'm going to ask you to introduce
yourself and then I have a number of

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.710
questions that I would like to ask.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:35.235
...I want to start with the word that
horrifies all the beginners in Russian.

00:00:35.805 --> 00:00:37.695
Uh, so yeah, I'm a teacher.

00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:41.595
You said I'm an online teacher
about also, I am a teacher at

00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:43.695
the university at CU Boulder.

00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:51.435
Uh, so I teach offline, I teach online
and I try to find this balance to find

00:00:51.435 --> 00:00:57.150
this perfect way of learning Russian
for people who learn with teacher,

00:00:57.599 --> 00:01:02.610
with a teacher, for people who learn,
um, by themselves, on their own.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:06.200
So that's, that's pretty
much what I try to do.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:08.070
Okay.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.865
We will leave a link to your YouTube
channel in the description box.

00:01:12.315 --> 00:01:20.205
Um, so there was a very famous Hungarian
polyglot Kato Lomb or Lomb Kato who said,

00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:25.215
who had a formula for language learning,
which essentially was that it's a, it's a

00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:31.770
function of the motivation of the learner,
the time put in divided by inhibition,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.790
frustration, difficulty and everything
that reduces the level of motivation.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.650
So my first question is how do
you, what do you do to increase the

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.860
motivation for people to learn Russian?

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.960
And what do you do to decrease the
difficulties, real or imagined, that

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.340
are there in the Russian language?

00:01:51.225 --> 00:01:56.895
Oh, first of all, I think the most
important thing is to use the materials

00:01:57.015 --> 00:01:59.475
that are interesting for students.

00:01:59.955 --> 00:02:05.775
It's hard to do it online, but
offline I always try to ask in

00:02:05.775 --> 00:02:08.950
a very first, uh, how to say?

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.680
On the very  first lesson, I try to
ask some personal questions: what they

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.210
like, what they are interested in,
like somebody, uh, can be interested

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:23.880
in um, you know, science, chemistry,
somebody might like motorcycles.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.570
If somebody is a fan of Harry
Potter or Lord of the Rings

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.060
or Star Wars or anything.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.430
So at the very beginning, when people
cannot deal with authentic Russian

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:42.679
materials as much, I try to use
something they like uh, in Russian, for

00:02:42.679 --> 00:02:48.619
example, we are going to use Star Wars
characters in Russian, making dialogues

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:55.399
between, I don't know, Darth Vader and
Yoda, completely made up of course.

00:02:56.185 --> 00:03:00.745
Um, so something that would keep
people engaged rather than using

00:03:00.745 --> 00:03:05.765
random characters, like some Sylvia
from Italy comes to Russia and

00:03:05.795 --> 00:03:07.614
interacts or something like that.

00:03:07.614 --> 00:03:13.825
So when it's something, uh, more funny,
uh, it's a, it's just more engaging

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:18.204
and I encourage people when they learn
on their own to do the same thing,

00:03:18.204 --> 00:03:23.265
rather than using something that
they don't care about like articles

00:03:23.265 --> 00:03:25.665
about weather or something like that.

00:03:26.265 --> 00:03:27.954
Use the same materials...

00:03:27.954 --> 00:03:32.565
I, I just want to pick up on that because
I think you make a very important point.

00:03:33.105 --> 00:03:37.274
So many of these beginner books talk
about, you know, Mario comes from the

00:03:37.274 --> 00:03:41.204
Middle East, at the train station he's
with, you know, customs, uh, he goes

00:03:41.204 --> 00:03:44.835
to the bank, he goes to the doctor and
all of these are totally uninteresting.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.660
And typically the narrator
is bored reading the story.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.730
And so that's a very bad place to start.

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:55.380
And what you're suggesting is things
that are not only of interest to your

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.120
learners, but also familiar to them.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.930
I think it's very important to deal
with material that is familiar.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.410
And another problem, sometimes in language
texts they go to the very esoteric,

00:04:05.665 --> 00:04:09.775
cultural aspects of Korean or Japanese
or Russian, whereas really they should

00:04:09.775 --> 00:04:13.405
be beginning with, as you said, you
know, Harry Potter or Star Wars or,

00:04:13.555 --> 00:04:15.325
or something that is already familiar.

00:04:15.415 --> 00:04:18.835
So we can go from the familiar to
the less familiar because already the

00:04:18.835 --> 00:04:20.685
language is of course not familiar.

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:22.145
Yes.

00:04:22.474 --> 00:04:23.835
I totally agree with what you said.

00:04:24.465 --> 00:04:29.685
And you mentioned boring narrators, this
is a huge problem because especially

00:04:29.685 --> 00:04:35.265
those older books that have audio with
them, it seems as if, as if some robots

00:04:35.265 --> 00:04:38.414
were reading those texts, it is awful.

00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:43.485
So in my podcasts, in my I'll
do lessons, I always try to

00:04:43.485 --> 00:04:46.125
use as many emotions as I can.

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:49.905
So when it's like
whispering or yelling or...

00:04:50.025 --> 00:04:55.335
so I try to be engaging because when it's
an audio lesson, it's even more difficult

00:04:55.335 --> 00:05:01.075
to, to keep your attention there because
you don't see the person speaking.

00:05:01.075 --> 00:05:07.735
So those, um, monotonous and boring
readings, it's a very discouraging.

00:05:07.825 --> 00:05:08.965
So yes, emotion...

00:05:09.025 --> 00:05:11.965
I checked out your videos by the way,
and they are excellent and they're

00:05:11.965 --> 00:05:15.115
very lively and that's exactly
what people need to motivate them.

00:05:15.775 --> 00:05:16.555
And we learn better.

00:05:16.555 --> 00:05:17.425
There's more resonance.

00:05:17.425 --> 00:05:18.175
We learn better.

00:05:18.895 --> 00:05:19.375
Yeah.

00:05:19.375 --> 00:05:23.695
So because, uh, otherwise you'll
just fall asleep after what?

00:05:23.695 --> 00:05:26.485
10, 10 minutes into the, into the Russian.

00:05:27.975 --> 00:05:33.735
Uh, so you have materials that are
interesting, being more emotional

00:05:33.735 --> 00:05:37.015
and, uh, uh, pleasure, first of all.

00:05:37.035 --> 00:05:41.595
So as soon as you feel discouraged, as
soon as you feel overwhelmed, you feel

00:05:41.605 --> 00:05:44.175
that, uh, I'm not having any progress.

00:05:44.175 --> 00:05:44.805
I am at this...

00:05:45.875 --> 00:05:48.085
how to pronounce?

00:05:48.664 --> 00:05:49.125
Plateau.

00:05:49.145 --> 00:05:50.685
In Russian we say...

00:05:50.685 --> 00:05:52.585
Yhe plateau, yeah.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:52.915
Yes.

00:05:53.615 --> 00:05:54.115
Yes.

00:05:54.185 --> 00:05:57.065
And it's an inevitable, it's inevitable.

00:05:57.485 --> 00:06:01.475
Uh, and a lot of people are so
discouraged that they think that

00:06:01.505 --> 00:06:03.425
this is the end and they just quit.

00:06:03.725 --> 00:06:07.205
And in reality, it's just a milestone.

00:06:07.325 --> 00:06:13.235
You need to overcome this point and you
will maybe skyrocket on the next stage.

00:06:13.745 --> 00:06:16.445
So it's very important
to keep your motivation.

00:06:17.684 --> 00:06:22.094
While on the subject of the plateau,
uh, how do you deal with the experience

00:06:22.094 --> 00:06:25.155
that I had with Russian, and that is
that no matter how many times you look

00:06:25.155 --> 00:06:30.015
at the declension table, no matter how
many times you look at the endings in

00:06:30.015 --> 00:06:34.515
the three different genders, for all the
different cases, you can't remember them.

00:06:35.085 --> 00:06:40.034
You, you, maybe you can identify if
you're reading from the context, you

00:06:40.034 --> 00:06:42.075
can identify the meaning of that word.

00:06:42.075 --> 00:06:47.865
But when you're speaking to try to get
the uh, case ending, no matter how much

00:06:47.865 --> 00:06:49.515
you study, it just seems so elusive.

00:06:49.815 --> 00:06:53.835
How do you help your Russian learners
improve in their use of cases?

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:00.690
Oh, first of all, I try to be very nice
and especially in the first stages,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:06.419
because I learned this mostly from my
American students before I used to be

00:07:06.419 --> 00:07:11.760
this harsh Russian person who corrects
all mistakes, "you should be perfect!"

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:15.750
It was a long time ago, but that's
how it started, unfortunately.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:22.045
And then several people told me
that I made them cry after I was

00:07:22.045 --> 00:07:25.405
in my nice, I was in my nice stage.

00:07:25.435 --> 00:07:28.795
I thought I was encouraging, but
some people told me that they

00:07:28.795 --> 00:07:32.575
were so frustrated even with minor
corrections at the beginning that

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:38.335
they were crying and it turned my
whole understanding of it upside down.

00:07:38.335 --> 00:07:43.420
So after that, I am the nicest
teacher ever at the beginning.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.090
Of course, uh, I never correct.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.320
Never correct people.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:54.159
I'm happy when they say anything
to me later when I hear them being

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:58.570
confident already, but when they
make mistakes, I will correct them.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.690
And then we will concentrate
on grammatical aspects of that.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:06.990
But at the beginning, it's important
just to try because no harm done

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:11.215
if you mix those endings of cases.

00:08:11.215 --> 00:08:12.265
It's very confusing.

00:08:12.475 --> 00:08:13.555
Even for Russian people.

00:08:13.555 --> 00:08:14.515
It's very confusing.

00:08:14.935 --> 00:08:18.865
And we learn cases at Russian
schools being native speakers

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:20.725
still for several years.

00:08:20.725 --> 00:08:24.265
We'll learn those endings,
those questions for the cases.

00:08:24.265 --> 00:08:25.765
It's, it's a difficult thing.

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:27.955
We can't underestimate it.

00:08:28.525 --> 00:08:32.455
So yeah, just slowly try...

00:08:33.175 --> 00:08:36.255
I think that's a very important
point that you just made.

00:08:36.584 --> 00:08:40.304
There is a stage in our learning where
we want to be corrected, but at the

00:08:40.304 --> 00:08:42.375
early stages, we need encouragement.

00:08:42.405 --> 00:08:47.464
We need encouragement to keep speaking
and many mistakes do correct themselves.

00:08:48.105 --> 00:08:51.015
Conversely many of the
corrections that the teacher

00:08:51.015 --> 00:08:52.545
makes have no effect whatsoever.

00:08:52.995 --> 00:08:57.135
Uh, I know that, uh, in different
languages, if I look at my sort of

00:08:57.195 --> 00:09:01.695
lesson notes I get from my teacher,
I make the same mistakes every week.

00:09:01.965 --> 00:09:06.645
So it's, it's a gradual process,
combination of reading the rules

00:09:06.645 --> 00:09:10.845
and the teacher correcting you and
self-correcting, but it certainly,

00:09:10.875 --> 00:09:13.665
there's no quick solution to these things.

00:09:14.025 --> 00:09:16.985
And you just have to hope that
eventually the brain gets used to them.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:19.950
But, uh, cases are a problem in Russian.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:23.910
And how about the other difficulty
in Russian, which is this whole

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.970
aspect of verbs, verbs of emotions.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:31.430
Do you have any sort of secret
in helping people with this?

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.910
For verbs, especially verbs of
motion, verbs with prefixes I

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.470
have a special course for that.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:42.120
I try again to put them
into some funny context.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:48.660
I create stories again, using Harry Potter
or using some famous actors, uh, using,

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.720
uh, some popular, popular characters.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:58.130
And I try to put those verbs into a
funny context, funny mini story, and

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:07.405
then I will ask simple questions for
every sentence, like  ...  and, uh,

00:10:09.645 --> 00:10:13.965
for 20 minutes straight, just repeating
the same stuff again and again.

00:10:14.295 --> 00:10:21.615
And, um, since mostly those prefaces keep
their meaning for most of the verbs after

00:10:21.645 --> 00:10:26.995
10 mini stories like that, this is, this
just gets somewhere deep in your memory.

00:10:26.995 --> 00:10:32.385
And next time, just click and you
use it naturally because you heard

00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:34.515
it and repeated it so many times.

00:10:34.545 --> 00:10:41.115
You just have the feeling of when
you want to go out, you say ... so

00:10:41.485 --> 00:10:45.645
it's just the point of practice and
hearing the same thing again and

00:10:45.645 --> 00:10:50.355
again, in the same situations, but
with different different characters.

00:10:51.225 --> 00:10:55.065
And, and there's no predicting when
all of a sudden we start being able

00:10:55.065 --> 00:10:56.905
to use some of these things correctly.

00:10:57.314 --> 00:11:01.305
So sometimes it's a bit, it's the same
as trying to remember words, certain

00:11:01.305 --> 00:11:03.224
vocabulary items just never stick.

00:11:03.734 --> 00:11:07.574
And we shouldn't allow ourselves
to be frustrated and the same way

00:11:07.625 --> 00:11:10.665
with grammar, we shouldn't allow
ourselves to be frustrated at some

00:11:10.665 --> 00:11:12.974
point it will probably click in.

00:11:13.515 --> 00:11:18.314
And, uh, I think your, your approach
of patience, repetition, but not

00:11:18.314 --> 00:11:19.785
being too demanding of people.

00:11:20.145 --> 00:11:21.224
It makes a lot of sense.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.840
Um, one final note.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.100
So I think, uh, if we...

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:30.810
today in the world, if we look
at the most popular languages,

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:31.910
you know, languages that are...

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:35.550
that people are learning, obviously
English is probably number one

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.040
because of its practical usefulness.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:44.670
And then historically certain European
languages like Spanish are popular.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:48.900
And, uh, recently Korean and Japanese
have become popular for reasons

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:50.910
related to pop culture and so forth.

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.840
I mean, I happen to be a
fan of the 19th century.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:56.940
So Russia, for me, it was very attractive.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:02.430
Uh, and of course, Russia is an enormous
country with, uh, not only, you're not

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.700
only sort of the original Slavic Russian
people, but it's the whole influence

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:07.590
of central Asia and the Caucuses.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.620
I mean, it's just a spectacular
world, this whole Russian world thing.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:11.490
That's me.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.700
What is most attractive to your students?

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:15.870
What brings them to Russian?

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:23.189
Oh today it's a very complicated
question because unfortunately what

00:12:23.189 --> 00:12:28.620
I notice in the past decade, uh,
the interest to the Russian language

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.140
is, is decreasing unfortunately.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:35.730
And, uh, more and more often each
new semester, the university, I

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:40.329
see that it's some people that are
interested in uh, military stuff.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:45.939
So there people in uniforms sitting
in class, I'm like, oh, okay.

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:51.430
It's not a good reason to learn
Russian, but, uh, but unfortunate...

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:55.300
so this is one reason, I guess a
lot of, uh, military people are

00:12:55.449 --> 00:12:58.390
suddenly very interested in Russia.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:07.110
Um, but peaceful people usually
are interested in, um, literature.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:12.180
I heard that, but this is a, this is
rare because people are realistic.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:18.690
They understand that, uh, from the point
of starting learning the language to the

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:24.900
point where they will be able to read
Dostoevsky or War and Peace, Tolstoy

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.199
in original, this, this is a huge way.

00:13:28.199 --> 00:13:30.930
So, um, what else?

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:35.189
A lot of people have parents from
Russian speaking countries from

00:13:35.189 --> 00:13:37.319
the former Soviet Union countries.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:40.949
And they start to forget the language.

00:13:40.979 --> 00:13:44.310
They start to speak with an
accent, or they confuse the words.

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:48.420
Some of those people
come a lot to my classes.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:51.180
A lot of people come
to just do it for fun.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:52.965
Yeah, that's true.

00:13:53.205 --> 00:13:57.195
I mean, maybe, maybe, uh, if there
were some, you know, Russian equivalent

00:13:57.195 --> 00:14:01.575
of anime or K-pop, that would
encourage people to learn Russian.

00:14:01.995 --> 00:14:05.745
Uh, just on the subject of, of,
okay, I said that I was interested

00:14:05.745 --> 00:14:08.865
in 19th century literature, but
I'm learning Arabic right now.

00:14:08.865 --> 00:14:15.915
And I came across this, uh, cartoon series
from Jordan, therefore in Levantine Arabic

00:14:16.185 --> 00:14:18.465
on YouTube three to five minutes long.

00:14:19.125 --> 00:14:22.395
Cartoons about this family
and there's three kids and the

00:14:22.395 --> 00:14:23.595
kids are teasing each other.

00:14:23.595 --> 00:14:28.694
And the typical struggles between husband
and wife and it's just so well done.

00:14:28.694 --> 00:14:35.805
And it's so amusing and it comes with
either English or, um, Arabic subtitles.

00:14:35.985 --> 00:14:37.845
And each episode is three to five minutes.

00:14:38.729 --> 00:14:42.449
And there's like an endless number of
these, and it's just struck me and I'm

00:14:42.449 --> 00:14:44.010
using them for my Levantine Arabic.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:47.099
And these are phenomenal for
language learning, phenomenal.

00:14:47.099 --> 00:14:49.920
You can watch it with English subtitles,
it's three or five minutes long.

00:14:50.099 --> 00:14:52.709
You can then read through the
dialogue which I do on LingQ.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.489
You can listen to it and
it's funny, it's familiar.

00:14:56.489 --> 00:14:57.719
It's a family situation.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:01.620
Doesn't matter whether you're in
Jordan or Russia or Canada, family

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.120
dynamics are kind of similar.

00:15:03.479 --> 00:15:08.069
So it's, it's a spectacular, uh,
learning, uh, material resource.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.120
Uh, I don't know whether anything
like that exists for Russian and

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.305
even the fact that it's it it's
cartoons makes it lighter and fun

00:15:16.365 --> 00:15:19.455
and that the voices, I mean, the
actors, you know, the voices are, are

00:15:19.455 --> 00:15:21.255
wonderful and it's just excellent.

00:15:21.255 --> 00:15:22.995
And it's, it's dialogue rich.

00:15:23.265 --> 00:15:25.965
Uh, there's no point in watching
a movie where there's lots of,

00:15:26.835 --> 00:15:31.845
you know, scenery and silence or,
or a shooting or a door slamming.

00:15:32.085 --> 00:15:35.715
You need like dialogue, rich
content that's amusing and familiar.

00:15:35.715 --> 00:15:37.155
And these certainly do that.

00:15:37.665 --> 00:15:40.445
Um, but I don't know if anything
similar exists for Russian.

00:15:41.555 --> 00:15:43.590
Yeah, we do have a lot
of Russian cartoons.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:47.220
They are originally made for Russians
of course, for Russian children.

